      "worked": "The negative-index law gives $2^{-x}=\\frac{1}{2^x}$. Multiply the numerator and denominator by $2^x$:\n\n$$\\begin{align*}f(x)&=\\frac{1}{1+\\frac{1}{2^x}}\\\\f(x)&=\\frac{2^x}{1+2^x}.\\end{align*}$$\n\nSubstituting $-x$ gives\n\n$$\\begin{align*}f(-x)&=\\frac{1}{1+2^{-(-x)}}\\\\f(-x)&=\\frac{1}{1+2^x}.\\end{align*}$$\n\nAdd the numerators over the common denominator:\n\n$$\\begin{align*}f(x)+f(-x)&=\\frac{2^x}{1+2^x}+\\frac{1}{1+2^x}\\\\f(x)+f(-x)&=\\frac{2^x+1}{1+2^x}\\\\f(x)+f(-x)&=1.\\end{align*}$$\n\nThis holds for all real $x$, since $2^x>0$ and the denominators are nonzero.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
